; TEST: abort=1
;
; `div i8 -128 -1`: the ONE overflow case a division has. -128 / -1 is 128,
; and no `i8` holds 128, so it traps -- kind 1, the shared `bfausr_trap`,
; the same kind `add i8 127 1` gets (trap_add_i8.ir).
;
; T_MIN IS i8's OWN, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE. The
; canonical form (ssa-ir.md 2.2) holds -128 sign-extended in a 64-bit
; slot, and the 64-bit `idiv` of -128 by -1 computes 128 without a murmur:
; the hardware's own divide-error case is INT64_MIN / -1, which this is
; not. A lowering that checked against INT64_MIN would let this through and
; return 7. So the emitted check is
;
;       mov     rdx, -128
;       xor     rdx, rax
;       not     rcx
;       or      rdx, rcx
;       jz      bfausr_trap
;
; with -128 built from the operand type's own N (emit.inc's
; __bfa_emit_divrem: `1 shl (N-1)`, negated).
;
; If nothing traps the function returns 7.
;
; Mutation (run): the T_MIN literal taken as INT64_MIN -> exit 7, nothing
; trapped, 128 sitting in an `i8` slot.
;
; NO `c-emit-exit=`: the C backend lowers `div` too (`exsi_div_u`/
; `exsi_div_i`, c-backend.md D4 row 4), so this fixture is BUILT four ways
; -- gcc and clang, -O0 and -O2 -- and each build must trap with the same
; kind on the same input. The helper's check PRECEDES its division: under
; `-fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all` a division that executed
; first would abort inside UBSan and never reach `exsrt_abortus`, which is a
; different program from the one this fixture pins.
; This is the fixture that pins T_MIN AT T'S OWN WIDTH in BOTH backends: the
; C helper compares against `0 - (UINT64_C(1) << (n - 1))` with the operand
; type's own n, so a helper that compared against INT64_MIN instead would
; return 7 here exactly as the mis-emitted reference would.

functio @initium (ptr) -> u8 {
b0:
%0 = param ptr 0
%1 = iconst i8 -128
%2 = iconst i8 -1
%3 = div i8 %1 %2
%4 = iconst u8 7
ret %4
}
